# Chapter00 canonical cutover evidence review

Reviewed implementation and public GitHub commit: 4895c1a6a94660a43e617ed86a21844838a7324d. The subsequent 6e7ab06197c412d28305a174a54deda355daa790 merge adds only 142 paths under conformance/results/doc-annotated-blocks; production, registry, chapter00 source and projection are unchanged. This review performed no production or manuscript edits. No new blocking finding was detected in the bounded chapter00 evidence.

The separate code review manifest is cd88bd8aecd08209692ee859e5bf4ffd621e467d6cbaee16628266344754dd6b. It binds the unchanged, previously independently reviewed 14026-byte manuscript (48c2b1696c7308ee43f26dbf06a6038b38f0199233c1da3ef15a04453f810e93), the historical converter input, preserved fixture expectations, source/alias/projection negative cases and exact production build dependencies. This cutover review does not replace the original full meaning/Ruby reviews or the other reviewer's new source/output content comparison.

## Actual generation and identities

All four root first/second generated files were independently compared byte for byte, and each of the three artifact hashes was checked against the output manifest. Then the reviewer independently rebuilt production executable from the frozen Git-blob workspace and ran doc-canonical html once. Its four output files, including Usage-bearing manifest, exactly match both root runs. Commands, binary SHA and result are in generation.json; the independent generated files are preserved here.

Chapter00 HTML is 38595 bytes, SHA256 9e20c1b7dbc830f4932fdf069414f56e724125e36f691c19948ed90d31e64815. Chapter13 HTML remains 91421 bytes, SHA256 99b80b99318619335eca6bebbcc2d0ec044f12c2c4fe35d5020dc53af4a355a1, and CSS remains SHA256 f95ac62e6da394ed4324588c749fc670ccc9244d9e54bc8e8a1e0d5046cdee2b. The common manifest changes appropriately for two registered pages. The 00 input is physical doc/spec/00-contract.nepld and logical source remains doc/spec/00-contract.md; route is docs/spec/00-contract.html. Parse/lower/output all use default finite limits, including Work100M, Allocation500M, Nodes10M, Output10M; initial Usage is zero. No increased-budget result was substituted.

## Public GitHub and local navigation

Independent Playwright 1.62.0 execution loaded the actual fixed public GitHub URL. Server rawLines agree with every generated line. Because rawLines alone do not prove terminal newline bytes, a separate request to the fixed raw.githubusercontent.com URL also compared the complete bytes, including terminal LF. Both raw projection and actual rendered HTML are retained.

All seven old fragment names and six semantic names are unique within the actual markdown article. Each of 13 tests starts with the target outside the viewport at the opposite end, navigates using the actual hash, then observes at least five consecutive stable scroll samples and a changed scroll position. The decoded hash must match. Final target position must be approximately GitHub's 125px sticky-header offset or the browser's document-end scroll clamp, not merely visible somewhere. All 13 passed in Chromium 151.0.7922.34. This establishes behavior for the fixed public commit, not future GitHub renderer behavior or a Pages deployment. Original root results are retained separately.

Local independent execution used the saved actual output, JavaScript disabled, Chromium 151.0.7922.34, Firefox 153.0 and WebKit 26.5, under /first/ and /second/ prefixes at 375 and 1280 pixels. All 12 configurations passed exact base-text heading checks, all ten code spellings, list item counts 4/6/14, exactly 277 Ruby nodes, unique semantic IDs, stylesheet loading, no script elements, ja article language and no horizontal page overflow. All 72 semantic navigations started with target outside the viewport, performed real hash movement and ended at the target's clamped document coordinate after at least five stable samples. Separate ARIA snapshots contain INV01 and INV14. Opening screenshots for WebKit375 and Chromium1280 were visually inspected; no obvious overlap was observed in these views. This is not a full-page visual or human screen-reader certification, and no keyboard-link traversal is claimed for this chapter without interactive links.

## Compiler-free restoration

The exact Python fence was extracted from the archive README and executed unchanged in an isolated sandbox with the copied payload index and four selected archive files. Each payload length and SHA was verified by that script; restored HTML/CSS/manifest are byte-identical to the independently generated four files. The snapshot was then opened via file URLs in all three engines with JavaScript disabled and HTTP(S) requests blocked. All three passed seven headings, 277 Ruby nodes, lists 4/6/14, the expected dark background and loaded adjacent stylesheet, without HTTP requests. No compiler, parser, Node or network generation is required to read this restored snapshot. bootstrap.json binds the precise README and payload-index revisions used; full archive verification is outside this four-payload restoration check.

## Limits and retained failures

The captured root logs show the accurately qualified historical Rust test ran 1 and passed, Python tests ran 4, tools unit tests ran 70 with one existing ignored, and both canonical checks passed. The original root unqualified --exact attempt selected zero tests and is not counted; the independent code review's initial incomplete scratch checkout failed before test execution and is preserved in that separate record. These records do not imply unexecuted whole-workspace, cross-target or exact-final-head CI success.

This is chapter00's source/alias/output/navigation/bootstrap evidence. It does not certify all documents, T21/T16 or J01-J04 globally, network destination availability, actual assistive-technology operation or public Pages deployment. The former Markdown remains only a deliberately pinned historical converter-test input; canonical metadata does not create two hand-maintained sources.

Raw root records, independent scripts/results, actual generated files and browser artifacts are preserved with hashes. Root browser scripts were read for their assertions, and independent scripts strengthen final-position checks. freeze.py captures live inputs and must not be rerun to overwrite this fixed record. Replay generation uses the separate fixed code-review workspace/binary build, while local.py and the saved snapshot can be replayed without Rust.
